From: meenmachine1 <78238681+meenmachine1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:13:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Decompiler: an option to print comparisons with the constant
 the code compares against

- Shows: `cmp r0,#0; ble` prints `x < 1`, and `cmp r0,#9; bge` prints
  `8 < x`, where the instruction (and usually the source) has `x <= 0`
  and `x >= 9`. Written back as C, the shifted constant is a different
  instruction (`cmp r0,#1; blt`), which a matching decompilation has to
  undo by hand, and the constant on the left reads backwards.
- Cause: RuleIntLessEqual (Funcdata::replaceLessequal) canonicalizes
  `V <= c` to `V < c+1`, also when a condition is negated
  (opFlipInPlaceExecute), and the printer prints the p-code's operand
  order, which for `x > c` has the constant first since p-code has no
  greater-than.
- Patch: replaceLessequal records the move on the op (a flag it toggles,
  so a constant moved twice, there and back, counts as unmoved). A new
  print option, `encodedcompare` (off by default), takes `right` or `on`.
  With `right`, a comparison against a constant prints with the constant
  on the right (`x > 8`), and a signed `-1 < x` as `x >= 0`. With `on`,
  also a comparison whose constant was moved in its less-or-equal form
  prints with the constant moved back (`x <= 0`, `x >= 9`). The p-code
  doesn't change, only how it prints; a constant with a symbol (an
  equate), a character, an enum, or one at its type's limit prints as
  before. `right` suits code from compilers that rewrite `x < 10` as
  `x <= 9` themselves (GCC, LLVM), whose instructions carry a constant
  the source doesn't; `on` a compiler that keeps the source's.
- Seen on: ARMv5TE (Nintendo DS, CodeWarrior) code in a matching
  decompilation; the same canonical forms on every processor.
- Ghidra 12.1.4.

